          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            “Transcendental syntax” (
            %a{:href => "#Girard13"}> Girard 13
            ) is the name of a proposal (or maybe a pamphlete) by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"} Jean-Yves Girard
            which means to rethink fundamental aspects of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            in the light of computer science (and more especially through the so-called
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curry-Howard+correspondence"} Curry-Howard-Lambek correspondence
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry+of+Interaction"} Geometry of Interaction
            are but exercises in transcendental syntax (
            %a{:href => "#Girard13b"}> Girard 13b
            ).
          %p
            While Girard’s prose is notoriously demanding, exegesis may be found in (
            %a{:href => "#AbrusciPistone12"}> Abrusci-Pistone 12
            ) for the philosophical side, (
            %a{:href => "#Rouleau13"}> Rouleau 13
            ) which was written before the more modern developements of transcendental syntax by means of stars and constellations (
            %a{:href => "#Girard13b"}> Girard 13b
            ) or (
            %a{:href => "#Eng23"}> Eng 23
            ) for an attempt to formalize and contextualize Girard’s last works from the point of view of computer science.
          %h3#getting_rid_of_semantics Getting rid of semantics
          %p
            Usually,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            dictates what logic is. It allows and forbids manipulations of symbols by following what we consider logic to be, in a dualism between symbol (interpreted) and its meaning (interpreter). An ambition of the transcendental syntax is to
            %em get rid of semantics
            (which is actually a distinguished form of syntax) by redirecting the computational power of semantics and its dialogue with syntax and putting it at the same level as syntax: the barrier is broken and everything becomes synctatic objects of same nature interacting in a same space from which logical meaning emerges. This approach is meant to make more explicit the mechanisms underlying logical reasoning (which appears in types, formulas, specifications, computer programs, mathematical proofs, computational constraints, computational complexity, …).
          %h2#philosophical_interpretation Philosophical interpretation
          %p
            The transcendental syntax starts from the claim that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            makes a lot of rather arbitrary choices such as the so-called
            %em rules of logic
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+systems"} deductive systems
          %p
            His point of view can be seen as an update of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kant"} Kant
            %em computation
            into account through recent works in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curry-Howard+correspondence"} Curry-Howard-Lambek correspondence
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Girard13b"} Girard 16
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th Analytic / Answers
                %th Synthetic / Questions
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong A posteriori / Explicit
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Constat
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Usine
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong A priori / Implicit
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Performance
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Usage
          %p
            The idea is that logic studies the relationship between questions (formulas being a special case) which are subjective and their answers (proofs being a special case) which are objective, by means of finite objects (because reasoning should be finite and verifiable, otherwise it would not even be possible). In order to be freed from those “prejudices”, one should start by defining the
            = succeed "," do
              %em answers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kant"} Kant
          %p
            The
            %strong answers
            correspond to the space of computation (generalizing proofs) which can be evaluated (
            %strong> performance
            ) into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+form"} normal form
            (
            %strong> constat
            ). This is the space where proofs are constructed but not yet considered logically meaningful or correct. It is the material on which logic is constructed.
          %p
            The space of
            %strong questions
            considers two alternative definitions of
            %em meaning
            corresponding to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formulas
            or
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} types
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the use (usage): it corresponds to
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ludwig+Wittgenstein"} Ludwig Wittgenstein
            %li
              %p
                the factory (usine): the meaning of computational objects is defined by a set of (well-chosen)
                %em tests
                they have to pass (exactly as for tests in programming).
          %p
            This is related to the distinction between existentialism and essentialism in philosophy.
            %strong Logical certainty
            corresponds to the
            %em adequacy
            between usine and usage: we would like factory’s tests to be finite and sufficient in a way to guarantee an associated computational behavior (in a same way tests in a factory should be relevant and guarantee a correct use of objets which are made, or in the case of programming, that the tests corresponding to some program specification guarantee some property like the absence of bugs).
          %p
            According to Girard
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Girard16"} Girard 16
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/incompleteness+theorem"} incompleteness theorem
            which intuitively implies that the possible uses of a logical object may go beyond what we expect from their definition (our formatting of the concept of logic).
          %p
            The transcendental syntax has a
            %em monist
            approach (opposed to a
            = succeed ")" do
              %em dualist
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} Curry-Howard-Lambek correspondence
          %h2#technical_interpretation Technical interpretation
          %p
            Technically speaking, the transcendental syntax is a finitary improvement of Girard’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry+of+Interaction"} Geometry of Interaction
            (in its original form and with its initial motivation). Some preliminary works include
            %em flows
            (
            %a{:href => "#Girard95"}> Girard 95
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Bagnol14"} Bagnol 14
            %em interaction graphs
            (
            %a{:href => "#Seiller16"}> Seiller 16
            ). The transcendental syntax generalizes and extends both by taking into account
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+net"} proof nets'
            logical correctness in a more general framework.
          %p
            The elementary objects (analytic) considered by Girard are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em constellations
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Robinson65"} Robinson 65
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+net"} proof structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+rule"} cut elimination
            but also the correction graphs asserting logical correctness. Constellations were initially found by analyzing the behavior of proof-nets and their correctness but other basis of computation can be chosen.
          %p Answers are constellations and questions correspond to two notions of types:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                by using
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizability
                techniques for linear logic as in ludics or Seiller’s interaction graphs, we can define
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formulas
                called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em behaviors
            %li
              %p
                Inspired by the logical correctness of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+net"} proof nets
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+net"} proof nets
          %p
            Although never explicitly mentioned by Girard, the transcendental syntax is very close to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizability theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Beffara06"} Beffara 06
          %h2#intuitive_illustrations Intuitive illustrations
          %h3#car_factory Car factory
          %p
            This is an example given by Girard in his book
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Girard16c"}
                %em “Le fantôme de la transparence”
          %p A car (analytics) can be certified by a factory (usine) and receive a label (type) which can guarantees that it will work as expected (adequacy). However, it would be absurd to drive 20000km with a car only to show that it can do it (actual use cannot serve as effective testing). Those factory tests are effective, finite, partial but sufficient. They are well-chosen and can be more laxist or strict.
          %h3#automata_theory Automata theory
          %p A finite automaton is a machine receiving a finite word as input and which either accepts or rejects it. Hence, there are two kind of objects (analytic): machines and words and machines can only interact with words.
          %p Only by interaction, a way to tell whether a machine recognizes some language is to feed it with all the words of that language (Girard’s use). However, in the case the language is infinite (e.g. all binary words ending with 00), you would never be able to conclude. However, we are still able to reason on automata and words by an external logical reasoning and analysis.
          %p The idea of the transcendental syntax would be to reify this external reasoning by extending the computational space such that it can express automata, words and their interaction but also such that it is “large” enough in order to introduce “exotic objects” which can serve as finite tests against automata, thus internalizing our external logical reasoning into the syntax observed.
          %p Those exotic objects are what Girard calls the “hidden files” of logic, in reference to hidden files in Unix systems which are often essential but invisible.
          %p This is reminiscent of how the space of real numbers is extended to complex numbers in order to solve some equations, or also of how the set of real numbers is a completion of rationals (where irrationnals are seen as such exotic elements).
          %h3#lambdacalculus Lambda-calculus
          %p
            Lambda-term is a syntactic theory of functions. In order to check whether some term is a function from some type
            %strong A
            to some type
            = succeed "," do
              %strong B
            %strong A
            and check whether it produces a term of type
            = succeed "." do
              %strong B
            = succeed "," do
              %strong A
          %p However, we are still able to say that a term is a function by using type rules for simply typed lambda-calculus (STLC). Those typing rules provide tests (Girard’s factory) and we have guarantees that if the term has the same of a function (it is a lambda abstraction) and that it is well-typed, then it will behave well as a function.
          %h2#axiomfree_systems Axiom-free systems
          %p
            Following this approach, it is theoretically possible (but non-trivial) to construct axiom-free systems. In (
            %a{:href => "#Girard20"}> Girard 20
            ), Girard roughly sketches a reconstruction of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
          %h2#transcendental_syntax_and_computer_science Transcendental syntax and computer science
          %p The transcendental syntax generalizes, extends or is simply related to several important aspects of computer science (although no direct link has been studied yet, thus making this section speculative):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+complexity"} descriptive complexity
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "#Girard18"} Girard 18
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/certified+programming"} model checking and program specification
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %em software testing
            %li
              %p
                Girard’s constellations are independent agents communicating with local interactions. This is reminiscent of the
                %em actor model
                in programming or of process calculi (such as the pi-calculus) and we can imagine applications to logic for concurrent, distributed or parallel systems. Links to
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yves+Lafont"} Yves Lafont
          %p
            Although no link have been properly studied, Girard’s constellations are reminiscent of biological or chemical computation. However, links between tile systems (Wang tiles and abstract tile assembly models) and Girard’s constellation have been mentionned in (
            %a{:href => "#Eng23"}> Eng 23
            ). Morever, it also has been remarked that constellations generalize a model called
            %em flexible tiles
            (
            %a{:href => "#JonoskaMcColm05"}> JonoskaMcColm 05
            ) which is used for DNA-based computation.
          %p The transcendental syntax may also serve as a way to develop new ideas and concepts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p in the same way as assembly languages are low-level languages close to machine operations, Girard’s constellations can be seen as a low-level language which would be closer to the elementary mechanisms of reasoning (since it decomposes lambda-terms) and be used as a compilation target.
            %li
              %p Since logical concepts emerge from objets without any logical meaning, the transcendental syntax may be used to design “logic-agnostic” proof-assistants or tools which are not bound to primitive definitions hard-coded into a compiler or an interpreter. Logical concepts would then be defined by the programmer (as programming libraries for instance).
